/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 111 0 R
/T (Why duplicates disappear: a set stores each element once, and uses hash + equality to decide whether an element is already present, so adding an existing value changes nothing. Why membership is faster: x in list compares x with every element, O\(n\); x in set hashes x and goes straight to its slot, O\(1\) average. Measured \(100 lookups of the last element\): 10,000 items 0.0091 s vs 0.000006 s; 100,000 items 0.130 s vs 0.000009 s; 1,000,000 items 1.67 s vs 0.000012 s. The list time grows 10x per step; the set time stays flat. Lists vs tuples in sets: set members must be hashable. A list is mutable and unhashable \(TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\); a tuple of hashable items is hashable and works \({\(1,2\),\(3,4\)}\).)
/E (Why duplicates disappear: a set stores each element once, and uses hash + equality to decide whether an element is already present, so adding an existing value changes nothing. Why membership is faster: x in list compares x with every element, O\(n\); x in set hashes x and goes straight to its slot, O\(1\) average. Measured \(100 lookups of the last element\): 10,000 items 0.0091 s vs 0.000006 s; 100,000 items 0.130 s vs 0.000009 s; 1,000,000 items 1.67 s vs 0.000012 s. The list time grows 10x per step; the set time stays flat. Lists vs tuples in sets: set members must be hashable. A list is mutable and unhashable \(TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\); a tuple of hashable items is hashable and works \({\(1,2\),\(3,4\)}\).)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [71]
>>
endobj
