894 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 114 0 R
/T (Timings \(seconds\): 10,000 readings - loop 0.0091, comprehension 0.0082, map/filter 0.0107; 100,000 - 0.0721, 0.0915, 0.0604; 1,000,000 - 0.513, 0.595, 0.646. The results are close and none always wins. Conclusion - is a list comprehension always best? No. It is concise and usually as fast as a loop, but in my measurements the comprehension version was not the fastest at 100,000 and 1,000,000 readings because it probably makes three passes over the data \(valid C, valid F, alarms\) while the loop does everything in one pass. Choose by readability and by whether you need all the results at once; use a generator when the data is large and processed once.)
/E (Timings \(seconds\): 10,000 readings - loop 0.0091, comprehension 0.0082, map/filter 0.0107; 100,000 - 0.0721, 0.0915, 0.0604; 1,000,000 - 0.513, 0.595, 0.646. The results are close and none always wins. Conclusion - is a list comprehension always best? No. It is concise and usually as fast as a loop, but in my measurements the comprehension version was not the fastest at 100,000 and 1,000,000 readings because it probably makes three passes over the data \(valid C, valid F, alarms\) while the loop does everything in one pass. Choose by readability and by whether you need all the results at once; use a generator when the data is large and processed once.)
