/S /H3
/P 117 0 R
/T (Timings for 200 random queries \(seconds\): 100 trains: scan 0.0069, index 0.0007; 1,000 trains: 0.0611 vs 0.0041; 10,000 trains: 0.666 vs 0.054 \(about 12x faster\). The scan grows about 10x for each 10x more trains, as O\(total stops\) predicts, while the index stays proportional to trains per station. Index memory: 34,472 B / 112,528 B / 873,488 B, plus a one-time build of 0.0083 s at 10,000 trains.)
/E (Timings for 200 random queries \(seconds\): 100 trains: scan 0.0069, index 0.0007; 1,000 trains: 0.0611 vs 0.0041; 10,000 trains: 0.666 vs 0.054 \(about 12x faster\). The scan grows about 10x for each 10x more trains, as O\(total stops\) predicts, while the index stays proportional to trains per station. Index memory: 34,472 B / 112,528 B / 873,488 B, plus a one-time build of 0.0083 s at 10,000 trains.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [30]
>>
endobj
1118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 117 0 R
/T (Conclusion: an index stores, for every station, the answer to 'which trains stop here?' in advance, so a query no longer has to read every train. The trade-off is extra memory \(about 0.87 MB here\), the time to build the index, and the need to update it whenever the timetable changes.)
/E (Conclusion: an index stores, for every station, the answer to 'which trains stop here?' in advance, so a query no longer has to read every train. The trade-off is extra memory \(about 0.87 MB here\), the time to build the index, and the need to update it whenever the timetable changes.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [31]
>>
